  "final_text": "I did all four steps and checked the results by reading them back.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" covers the `kibana_sample_data_logs` index, and its time field is `timestamp`. The response code is stored as text in `response`, with an exact-match copy in `response.keyword`. Other fields include `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.*`, `host`, `machine.os`, `request`, `url` and `@timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-176f66d8 Web logs\" (id `s4-176f66d8-web-logs-2d45518f5914`) has exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart counting requests over `timestamp`.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart counting requests for each value of `response.keyword`, showing the top 20 codes.\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-176f66d8 5xx errors\" (id `6ec9b4d6-070d-43e2-9c5a-64abf9710b59`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes. It matches every response code from \"500\" to \"599\". The codes are stored as text, so this is a text comparison, which is accurate because every code has three digits. I created the rule already enabled, so it didn't need a separate enable step.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** I renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\". It was renamed by rebuilding the panel with the same chart settings and the new title. Reading the dashboard again shows the new title, and reading the rule back shows it enabled with status \"ok\".\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
